{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WgJ4jEt3SaMos6C2Dgfshx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed imagined experiment results for all \"planned\" experiments in the experiment plan, under the \"best\" tier scenario. Each prediction needs a result_rationale explaining why that result would occur in this tier.\n\nLet me carefully go through each planned experiment and generate realistic predictions consistent with the \"best\" tier definition.\n\nKey parameters for \"best\" tier:\n- func_level_clf_f1: [86.5, 88.0]\n- func_level_cwm_f1: [86.5, 88.0]\n- func_level_delta_pp: [-0.5, 0.5]\n\n\n- func_level_f1_std_across_seeds: [0.1, 0.5]\n- repo_level_clf_f1: [70, 78]\n- repo_level_cwm_f1: [55, 65]\n- repo_level_delta_pp: [8, 18]\n- cwm_unparseable_rate_repo_pct: [15, 35]\n- trace_then_judge_vs_clf_delta_pp: [-2, 0]\n- rerepair_resolve_rate_delta_pct: [3, 8]\n- empirical_block_corr_rho: [0.3, 0.6]\n\nI've wrapped up the baseline experiments—the CWM and CLF models show nearly identical performance at the function level, which confirms there's no meaningful difference there. The regression analysis came back negative too, with only about 1% regression rate. Now I need to run predictions across the remaining experiments: multi-seed validation, trace-then-judge comparisons, trajectory-level fine-tuning, model scaling effects, diagnostic repair analysis, per-test metrics, oracle stratification, and block-level analysis.\n\nLooking at the multi-seed results, I'm running CLF and CWM across three different seeds to check robustness. The F1 scores stay tightly clustered around 87.3 for CLF and 87.2 for CWM with minimal variance, and the paired bootstrap test shows no statistical significance—this solidifies the equivalence claim at the function level.\n\nFor trace-then-judge, I'm testing whether having the model generate intermediate reasoning before predicting pass/fail helps, but early results suggest the direct approach still performs better or at least matches it.\n\nI also need to generate predictions for the remaining experiments: swebench per-test data, repo-level training effects, LLM judge baselines, and per-test majority voting approaches. The per-test diagnostic approach shows strong results—resolve rates jump from 28% baseline to 33% with targeted re-repair, with even bigger gains on multi-failure cases. The failure detection itself is precise (88% precision, 86% recall), and the advantage scales with problem complexity: negligible for single-failure cases but substantial when there are three or more failures. I'm also checking the actual error correlation from the trained model, which comes in around 0.4.\n\nNow looking at the SWE-bench dataset construction and training results. The per-test data was successfully built with 500 instances and 16 candidates each. When fine-tuning on this data, there's a notable gap between the two approaches—the classifier achieves 74% repo-level F1 while the code-writing model only reaches 60%, with a 25% unparseable rate dragging down its performance. For comparison, the zero-shot LLM baselines show the smaller model (Qwen3-8B) hitting around 65% trajectory-level and 55% per-test accuracy, while the larger variant (Qwen3-32B) improves to 72% and 62% respectively.\n\nWith majority voting using K=3 on the classifier model, I'm seeing modest gains of 1-2 percentage points in F1 score, pushing function-level performance to around 88.5-89%. Now I need to compile all these results into the final YAML output.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10697,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10697},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"sess